    "searchSnippet": "Operating System: Module 1 Kavitha D N, Asst. Prof., Dept. of CS&E, VVCE Page 1 Module 1 Chapter 1: Introduction \u2022 What is Operating System? : An Operating system is a program or software that manages the computer hardware. \u2022 It also provides a basis for application programs and acts as an intermediary between computer user and the computer hardware. Examples: Windows, Linux, Android, IoS, etc. \u2022 The OS has 3 main functions: 1. Manages the computer\u2019s resource such as CPU, memory, etc. 2. Establish a user interface, and 3. Execute and provide services for application software. 1.1 What Operating System Do: \u2022 A computer system can be divided roughly into 4 components: a) Hardware, b) Operating system, c) Application programs, and d) The users. Figure 1.1: Abstract View of the Components of a computer System. a) Hardware: the central processing unit (CPU), the memory, and input/output devices- provide the basic computing resources for the system. b) The operating system: controls and coordinates the use of the hardware among the various application programs for the various users. Operating System: Module 1 Kavitha D N, Asst. Prof., Dept. of CS&E, VVCE Page 2 c) The application programs: such as word processors, spreadsheets, compliers, and web browsers- defines the way in which these resources are used to solve user\u2019s computing problems. 1.1.1 User View: \u2022 The user\u2019s view of computer system varies according to the interface being used. 1) Most computer users uses PC, such systems are designed for one user to monopolize its resources. \u2022 The goal is to maximize the work that the user is performing. \u2022 In this case, the OS is designed for ease of use. 2) A user sits at a terminal connected to mainframe or minicomputer. Other users are accessing the same computer through other terminals. \u2022 These users share resources and may exchange information. \u2022 Here, OS is designed to maximize resource utilization. 3) User sits at workstations connected to networks of other workstations and servers. \u2022 Users have dedicated resources and also share resources such as networking and servers. \u2022 OS is designed to compromise between individual usability and resource utilization. 4) Most of the handheld computers are standalone units for individual users. \u2022 Some are connected to networks, either directly by wire or through wireless modems and networking. \u2022 OS is designed for individual usability, but performance per amount of battery life is",
